{{!
    @template local_byblos/docs/goals

    Guide: setting and tracking learning goals on the dashboard, and surfacing
    them on a page with the Goals section.

    Context variables:
    - links (object) Map of topic key => URL.
    - dashurl (string) Portfolio dashboard URL.
    - newpageurl (string) New-page wizard URL.

    @package    local_byblos
    @copyright  2026 South African Theological Seminary
    @license    http://www.gnu.org/copyleft/gpl.html GNU GPL v3 or later

    Example context (json):
    {
        "dashurl": "#",
        "newpageurl": "#",
        "pix": {"goals_tab": "#"},
        "links": {
            "index": "#", "getting_started": "#", "building_pages": "#",
            "publishing": "#", "collections": "#", "goals": "#", "sharing": "#",
            "assessment": "#", "pedagogy": "#", "best_practices": "#",
            "usecase_programme": "#", "usecase_course": "#",
            "usecase_professional": "#"
        }
    }
}}
<div class="byblos-docs-article">

    <h1>{{#str}}docs_topic_goals, local_byblos{{/str}}</h1>
    <p class="byblos-docs-lead">
        A portfolio shows what you have done. Goals add what you are working
        <em>towards</em>. You set a goal, track your progress, and link the evidence
        that shows you are getting there. This forward-looking habit is part of what
        makes an ePortfolio a tool for learning, not just a record of it (see
        <a href="{{{links.pedagogy}}}">Why ePortfolios work</a>).
    </p>

    <h2>Where your goals live</h2>
    <p>
        Your goals live on the <strong>{{#str}}tab_goals, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>
        tab of your <a href="{{{dashurl}}}">portfolio dashboard</a>. Each goal is yours
        alone: you create and manage your own, and a teacher can see them only where you
        choose to show them on a page. The same goals can then be displayed on any page
        using the <strong>{{#str}}sectiontype_goals, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> section.
    </p>

    <figure class="byblos-docs-figure">
        <img src="{{pix.goals_tab}}" class="byblos-docs-img" loading="lazy"
             alt="The Goals tab on the dashboard, with the quick-start starter goals and goal cards showing progress.">

        <figcaption>Figure 1: The Goals tab, where you set and track your goals.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Creating a goal</h2>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>Open the <strong>{{#str}}tab_goals, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> tab on your dashboard.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>{{#str}}goal_new, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>.</li>
        <li>Optional: pick a starter goal from the <strong>{{#str}}goal_startfrom, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>
            menu at the top of the form to prefill a research-backed goal, then edit it. Leave it on
            <strong>{{#str}}goal_startblank, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> to start from scratch.</li>
        <li>Give the goal a clear <strong>{{#str}}goal_title, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>, for example
            "Write with clearer structure" or "Become confident leading a small group".</li>
        <li>Add an optional <strong>{{#str}}goal_description, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> to say what
            success looks like, and an optional <strong>{{#str}}goal_target, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>
            if you are working to a deadline.</li>
        <li>Save. The goal appears as a card on the tab, starting at 0% and marked
            <strong>{{#str}}goalstatus_active, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="byblos-docs-tip">
        <strong>Write goals you can show evidence for.</strong> A good goal is specific enough that you can
        point to a piece of work and say "this shows progress". Vague goals are hard to evidence and hard to
        tell when you have met them.
    </div>

    <h2>Starter goals to adapt</h2>
    <p>
        Stuck for ideas? On the Goals tab, click <strong>{{#str}}goal_quickstart_toggle, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>
        to add any of these with a single click, then make it your own. Each one puts a research-backed
        <a href="{{{links.best_practices}}}">best practice</a> into action, the same conditions that make an
        ePortfolio a <a href="{{{links.pedagogy}}}">high-impact practice</a>. When you add one, decide what
        evidence would show you are getting there.
    </p>
    <ul class="byblos-docs-deflist">
        <li><strong>Make reflection a habit, not an afterthought.</strong> Reflection is the engine of
            portfolio learning. Evidence: a Reflection section added after each module, or a running
            learning-journal page.</li>
        <li><strong>Connect my learning across courses into one story.</strong> Integrating learning, rather
            than leaving it in separate course-shaped boxes, is one of the clearest benefits of a portfolio.
            Evidence: a collection with a home page that frames the throughline.</li>
        <li><strong>Map my evidence to my programme outcomes.</strong> Aligning evidence to outcomes
            (constructive alignment) makes your portfolio legible to an assessor. Evidence: an
            <strong>Outcome map</strong> section that links artefacts to each outcome.</li>
        <li><strong>Use feedback to revise, not just to receive it.</strong> Frequent, acted-on feedback is one
            of the conditions that make a practice high-impact. Evidence: a draft and a revised version that
            shows the changes you made in response to feedback.</li>
        <li><strong>Show growth over time, not just polished work.</strong> Evidence of change often
            demonstrates more learning than a single finished product. Evidence: an early attempt placed beside
            a later one, on a Timeline or a pair of pages.</li>
        <li><strong>Demonstrate my work to a real audience.</strong> Public demonstration of competence deepens
            the work. Evidence: a published <a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">showcase page</a> shared with a
            teacher, a cohort, or an employer, or a recorded presentation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Tracking your progress</h2>
    <p>Open a goal with its edit control to keep it up to date as you work:</p>
    <ul class="byblos-docs-deflist">
        <li><strong>{{#str}}goal_progress, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: a figure from 0 to 100 that fills
            the card's progress bar. Nudge it up as you make progress, rather than only at the end.</li>
        <li><strong>{{#str}}goalstatus_label, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: move a goal between
            <strong>{{#str}}goalstatus_active, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> (still working on it),
            <strong>{{#str}}goalstatus_achieved, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> (done), and
            <strong>{{#str}}goalstatus_archived, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> (set aside, kept for the record).</li>
        <li><strong>{{#str}}goal_target, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>: adjust or clear the deadline as your
            plans change.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Linking evidence to a goal</h2>
    <p>
        Evidence is what turns a goal from an intention into something you can demonstrate. You can attach
        any of your <a href="{{{links.building_pages}}}">artefacts</a> or pages to a goal:
    </p>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>On a goal card, click <strong>{{#str}}goal_addevidence, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>.</li>
        <li>Choose an <strong>{{#str}}goal_evidence_artefacts, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> item or one of your
            <strong>{{#str}}goal_evidence_pages, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> from the list.</li>
        <li>It appears under the goal as a link. Add as many pieces of evidence as you like, and remove any
            that no longer fit.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Showing your goals on a page</h2>
    <p>
        Goals are private to your dashboard until you choose to display them. To put them in front of a
        teacher or an assessor, add a <strong>{{#str}}sectiontype_goals, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong> section
        to a page (see <a href="{{{links.building_pages}}}">Building pages</a>):
    </p>
    <ol class="byblos-docs-steps">
        <li>In the page editor, add a section and choose <strong>{{#str}}sectiontype_goals, local_byblos{{/str}}</strong>.</li>
        <li>Choose whether to show <em>all your active goals</em> or only a <em>selection</em> you pick.</li>
        <li>The section shows each goal with its progress, updating automatically as you change the goal on
            your dashboard. There is nothing to keep in sync by hand.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="byblos-docs-note">
        <strong>Goals work well with reflection.</strong> Pair a Goals section with a
        <strong>Reflection</strong> section on the same page: state where you are heading, then reflect on the
        evidence that shows how far you have come. See <a href="{{{links.best_practices}}}">Best practices</a>
        for more on building a portfolio that shows growth, not just polish.
    </div>

    <h2>Next</h2>
    <p>
        With goals set and evidence linked, decide who can see your work in
        <a href="{{{links.sharing}}}">Sharing</a>, or build the page that will carry them in
        <a href="{{{links.building_pages}}}">Building pages</a>.
    </p>
</div>
